    {
        "id": 13,
        "slug": "c-adv-condition-variables",
        "lang": "c",
        "title": "Condition Variables",
        "explanation": "Condition variables let threads wait until a predicate becomes true, and let other threads signal them. Combined with a mutex, they build producer-consumer and other synchronization patterns.",
        "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <pthread.h>\n\nint ready = 0;\npthread_mutex_t mtx = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\npthread_cond_t cond = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;\n\nvoid *waiter(void *arg) {\n    (void)arg;\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&mtx);\n    while (!ready)\n        pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mtx);\n    printf(\"Waiter sees ready = %d\\n\", ready);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mtx);\n    return NULL;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    pthread_t t;\n    pthread_create(&t, NULL, waiter, NULL);\n\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&mtx);\n    ready = 1;\n    pthread_cond_signal(&cond);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mtx);\n\n    pthread_join(t, NULL);\n    return 0;\n}",
        "code_breakdown": "The waiter locks, checks the predicate ready, and waits if false — releasing the mutex while sleeping. The main thread sets ready and signals, waking the waiter, which rechecks and proceeds.",
        "output": "Waiter sees ready = 1",
        "output_explanation": "The while loop (not if) guards against spurious wakeups. After signal, the waiter reacquires the mutex, sees ready=1, and exits the loop.",
        "docs_url": "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/pthread_cond_wait.3.html",
    },
