IELTS_WRITING_PRACTICE_P2 = [
    {
        "id": 2,
        "title": "Line Graph — Copper, Nickel & Zinc Prices (2014)",
        "png": "static/images/Line Graph — CopperNickelZinc prices (2014).webp",
        "intro": "The line graph illustrates the average monthly percentage change in the prices of three metals — copper, nickel, and zinc — throughout the year 2014. Overall, all three metals experienced significant price fluctuations, with nickel showing the greatest volatility, while all three metals ended the year with positive monthly price changes in December.",
        "band5": "The line graph shows the average monthly change in prices of copper, nickel, and zinc in 2014. The Y-axis shows the percentage change compared with the previous month. Overall, nickel changed the most, and all metals ended with positive numbers in December.\n\nIn January, nickel had the highest price change at 6%. It fell to 4% in February and then down to 1% in March and April. In June, nickel dropped a lot to -3%, which was the lowest point on the graph. After June, it stayed at -1% from July to September. In October it went to -2%, but in November and December it rose to 1%.\n\nCopper started at 2% in January. It went down slowly to -0.5% in May and June. Then copper went back up to 1% in July, August, and September. In October, it was 0.5% and finished at 1.5% in December. Zinc started at 1% in January, went up to 3% in February, but fell to -1% in June. From July to September it stayed at -0.5%. In November and December, zinc increased to 1.5% and 2%.",
        "band6": "The line graph depicts the monthly percentage changes in the prices of three metals — copper, nickel, and zinc — over the year 2014. Overall, all three metals showed varying price changes across the 12 months, with nickel experiencing the most dramatic fluctuations, whereas copper remained relatively stable.\n\nNickel recorded the highest initial monthly increase in January at 6%. However, this percentage dropped steadily to 1% in March and April before plunging to a period low of -3% in June. Between July and September, nickel prices continued to fall by 1% each month, followed by another drop of 2% in October. Nevertheless, nickel prices recovered in November and December, recording a 1% monthly increase.\n\nRegarding zinc and copper, zinc's monthly change peaked at 3% in February before declining to -1% in June and remaining at -0.5% through September. By December, zinc rebounded to a 2% monthly increase. Copper followed a smoother trend, starting at 2% in January, dipping slightly to -0.5% in May and June, and fluctuating between 0.5% and 1.5% over the remaining months of the year.",
        "band7": "The line graph details the average monthly percentage price shifts for three different metals (copper, nickel, and zinc) over the course of 2014. Overall, all three commodities experienced considerable price instability, with nickel displaying the most severe fluctuations, while all three metals finished the year in positive growth territory by December.\n\nNickel began the year with the steepest monthly increase among all three metals, standing at 6% in January. It then entered a sharp decline, reaching 1% in March and eventually bottoming out at -3% in June, representing the lowest overall figure on the graph. Following three months of negative monthly growth at -1% from July to September and a further dip to -2% in October, nickel bounced back to register a 1% gain in both November and December.\n\nIn contrast, copper exhibited relatively moderate fluctuations. Starting with a 2% rise in January, it gradually decreased to -0.5% in May and June. It then recovered to stabilize at a 1% monthly gain from July to September, ending the year with a 1.5% increase in December. Meanwhile, zinc's monthly price change peaked at 3% in February before dropping into negative territory between June (-1%) and October (-1%). Zinc concluded the year with the strong monthly growth of 2% in December.",
        "band9": "The line graph delineates the month-on-month percentage changes in the prices of copper, nickel, and zinc throughout 2014. Overall, all three metals experienced marked volatility over the 12-month period. Nickel demonstrated the highest degree of price fluctuation, whereas copper was the least volatile; notably, despite significant mid-year downturns, all three commodities concluded the year with positive monthly price adjustments.\n\nNickel commenced 2014 with a substantial monthly price surge of 6% in January, outperforming the other two metals. However, its rate of growth rapidly decelerated to 1% by March, before plunging into negative territory to reach an overall low of -3% in June. Nickel prices continued to contract between July and September (at -1% monthly) and October (-2%), before mounting a minor recovery to post a 1% monthly increase in both November and December.\n\nConversely, copper and zinc followed somewhat less volatile trajectories. Zinc's monthly price growth peaked at 3% in February, after which it drifted into negative values from June (-1%) through October (-1%), before experiencing a sharp rebound to end the year at 2% in December. Copper remained the most stable overall, starting with a 2% price increase in January, dipping briefly to -0.5% mid-year (May and June), and maintaining steady positive growth of 1% to 1.5% across most of the second half of the year.",
        "tips": [
            "Understand the metric: the Y-axis measures monthly percentage change, not the actual price level.",
            "Negative values mean prices fell month-on-month; use terms like contracted, dipped into negative territory, or bottomed out.",
            "Describe volatility with words like fluctuated, marked volatility, or unstable.",
            "Group mid-year lows together when all lines dip at the same time for a more cohesive answer.",
            "When values stay flat across several months, state the date range instead of writing every single month."
        ],
        "examples": [
            {"sentence": "Nickel was 6% in January and went down to -3% in June.", "band": "Band 5", "tip": "Say 'increased by 6%' or 'recorded a 6% monthly rise' — the figure is a rate of change, not a flat value."},
            {"sentence": "Nickel recorded the highest initial monthly increase in January at 6%, but plunged to a period low of -3% in June.", "band": "Band 7", "tip": "Frame extreme points as 'period high' or 'period low' to add analytical detail."},
            {"sentence": "Nickel commenced 2014 with a substantial monthly price surge of 6% in January, before plunging into negative territory to reach an overall low of -3% in June.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Combine the initial peak and the later crash into one complex sentence using subordinate clauses."},
            {"sentence": "Zinc went up to 3% in February and then dropped.", "band": "Band 6", "tip": "Use formal peak vocabulary like 'peaked at' or 'reached a high of'."},
            {"sentence": "Zinc's monthly price growth peaked at 3% in February, after which it drifted into negative values before experiencing a sharp rebound to end the year at 2% in December.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Connect a line's trajectory using phrases like 'after which it drifted into X before rebounding to Y'."}
        ],
    },
    {
        "id": 3,
        "title": "Process Diagram — Rain-Shadow Desert Formation",
        "png": "static/images/Process — Rain-shadow desert.jpg",
        "intro": "The diagram illustrates the sequential natural process of how rain-shadow deserts are formed across geographical regions spanning thousands of kilometers. Overall, the process consists of seven distinct stages, beginning with moist air moving off the sea and ending with dry winds creating arid conditions on the inland side of a mountain range.",
        "band5": "The diagram shows how rain-shadow deserts are formed in seven steps. Overall, it starts from sea winds and ends with dry desert inland.\n\nFirst, in step 1, winds approach the coast from the sea. After that in step 2, winds are pushed upwards by the mountain. Next in step 3, moist air rises up and cools down. Because of cooling, in step 4, clouds form near the windward side of the mountain. Then in step 5, rain falls from these clouds on the mountain side.\n\nAfter rain falls, in step 6, dry air continues over the mountain top to the leeward side. Finally in step 7, dry winds reach inland areas. Because there is no water left in the air, a rain-shadow desert is created over thousands of kilometers. This process repeats over time.",
        "band6": "The diagram depicts the step-by-step process of rain-shadow desert formation. Overall, the natural cycle involves seven main stages, moving from the ocean across a mountain range to form dry desert conditions on the opposite inland side.\n\nInitially, winds blow from the sea towards the coast. As these winds strike the coastline, they are pushed upwards against the windward side of the mountain. During the third stage, this moist air ascends and cools down, which subsequently leads to cloud formation. Once the clouds are fully formed, rain falls heavily on the windward slope of the mountain.\n\nFollowing the precipitation stage, the air loses its moisture and continues moving over the peak of the mountain as dry air. In the final stage, these dry winds descend down the leeward side and reach the inland region. Over a span of thousands of kilometers, these moisture-deprived winds cause arid conditions, ultimately creating a rain-shadow desert.",
        "band7": "The diagram details the geographical mechanism behind the formation of rain-shadow deserts. Overall, it is a multi-step natural process comprising seven key stages, divided between the precipitation phase on the ocean-facing windward side and the desiccation phase on the inland leeward side.\n\nTo begin with, oceanic winds travel toward the coastline, where they are forced upward upon encountering elevated terrain. As this moist air ascends the windward slope, it gradually cools down, facilitating the condensation process that leads to cloud creation. Subsequently, heavy rain falls from these clouds, releasing most of the air mass's moisture on this side of the mountain.\n\nOnce the moisture is depleted, the remaining dry air continues its trajectory over the mountain peak toward the leeward side. As these dry winds descend into inland territory, they strip the land of humidity across an expanse of thousands of kilometers. Consequently, this persistent lack of rainfall results in the development of a rain-shadow desert.",
        "band9": "The diagram delineates the seven-stage meteorological process responsible for the creation of rain-shadow deserts over vast continental expanses. Overall, the phenomenon is driven by the movement of maritime air masses across high mountain barriers, resulting in heavy precipitation on the windward slope and extreme aridity on the leeward inland region.\n\nThe process commences when moist oceanic winds blow toward the coast. Upon hitting the coastline, these wind currents are deflected upward by the mountain range. As the rising air cools down, condensation occurs, giving rise to cloud formation along the windward slope. This is immediately followed by precipitation, during which the clouds release their moisture content onto the ocean-facing side of the mountain.\n\nHaving shed its water content, the air mass progresses over the summit as dry air. It then descends along the leeward slope, with dry winds ultimately sweeping across the inland areas. Over a geographical scale spanning thousands of kilometers, these moisture-stripped winds prevent rain formation, giving rise to a permanent rain-shadow desert.",
        "tips": [
            "Use the passive voice throughout a process essay to keep an objective, academic style.",
            "Master diverse sequential linkers: subsequently, following this, upon encountering, once the moisture is shed.",
            "Learn key topographical terms like windward side and leeward side to boost your Lexical Resource.",
            "Group stages into logical phases instead of mechanically listing steps 1 through 7.",
            "Paraphrase action verbs — replace 'rain falls' with precipitation and 'dry air' with moisture-deprived air mass."
        ],
        "examples": [
            {"sentence": "Winds are pushed upwards by the mountain.", "band": "Band 6", "tip": "Expand the sentence with cause and effect: 'Oceanic winds are forced upward upon encountering elevated terrain.'"},
            {"sentence": "As this moist air ascends the windward slope, it gradually cools down, facilitating cloud creation.", "band": "Band 7", "tip": "Use participle clauses like 'cools down, facilitating' to join consecutive steps smoothly."},
            {"sentence": "As the rising air cools down, condensation occurs, giving rise to cloud formation along the windward slope.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Introduce formal domain vocabulary like condensation or precipitation even when the diagram uses simpler terms."},
            {"sentence": "Then dry air goes over mountain and reaches inland.", "band": "Band 5", "tip": "Replace generic verbs like 'goes' with directional verbs such as descends, progresses, sweeps, or traverses."},
            {"sentence": "Having shed its water content, the air mass progresses over the summit as dry air, descending along the leeward slope.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Master structures like 'Having done X, Y occurs' for Band 9 grammatical range on process tasks."}
        ],
    },
    {
        "id": 4,
        "title": "Maps — Beechwood Farm (1950 vs Today)",
        "png": "static/images/Maps — Beechwood Farm (beforeafter).png",
        "intro": "The two maps illustrate the main changes that have taken place at Beechwood Farm between 1950 and the present day. Overall, the farm has shifted from a purely agricultural site to a commercial and tourist-friendly area, with the introduction of eco-friendly energy, visitor accommodations, and paved infrastructure.",
        "band5": "The two maps show Beechwood Farm in 1950 and today. Overall, the farm changed a lot from agricultural land to tourism and new facilities.\n\nIn 1950, there was a big area for sheep in the north side. Next to the river, there were areas for fruit trees, soft fruits, and vegetables. In the east, there was a barn, a farmhouse, and a chicken area. A simple track connected the road to the sheep field and the barn.\n\nToday, many things are modern. The sheep field is removed and replaced with a camping field and solar panels. Near the top road, a new farm shop and a parking area are built. The old track is now a proper road. The barn is moved to the middle area, and the old barn position is now holiday cottages. The farmhouse, chickens, fruit trees, soft fruits, and vegetables stay in the same place.",
        "band6": "The maps depict the transformation of Beechwood Farm over a period starting from 1950 up to the present day. Overall, the layout of the farm has undergone significant development, shifting from an agricultural farm into a commercial site with renewable energy and holiday facilities.\n\nIn 1950, the northern side of the farm was largely occupied by sheep. Along the river on the western boundary, there were dedicated plots for fruit trees, soft fruits, and vegetables. To the east, the property contained a barn, a farmhouse, and a chicken enclosure. A basic dirt track connected these areas to the main road at the top.\n\nLooking at the present-day layout, the sheep area has been replaced by a camping field and a set of solar panels in the northeast corner. Additional infrastructure for visitors has been added, including a farm shop and two parking areas. The original track has been upgraded into a paved road, while the barn has been relocated southwards. Holiday cottages now stand where the old barn was situated. However, the farmhouse, chicken area, and crop fields remain unchanged.",
        "band7": "The two maps compare the structural layout of Beechwood Farm as it was in 1950 with its modern configuration. Overall, the farm has undergone extensive modernization, evolving from a traditional farming site into a multi-purpose location that incorporates eco-tourism, retail facilities, and renewable energy, while retaining several original agricultural zones.\n\nIn 1950, the land was predominantly used for agricultural purposes. The northern area was allocated for sheep grazing, while the region alongside the river was split into dedicated sections for fruit trees, soft fruits, and vegetables. The southeastern portion housed a barn, a farmhouse, and an enclosure for chickens, all accessed by a simple unpaved track connected to the main road.\n\nAt present, substantial structural alterations are evident. The sheep grazing ground has been converted into a camping field, with solar panels installed in the top right corner to generate green energy. To accommodate tourists, two parking areas and a farm shop have been constructed. Furthermore, the track has been paved into a road network, and the barn has been moved south. The space previously occupied by the barn now features holiday cottages, whereas the crop fields, farmhouse, and chicken enclosure remain in their original locations.",
        "band9": "The two maps illustrate the structural evolution of Beechwood Farm from 1950 to the present day. Overall, the property has undergone a profound functional transformation, shifting from a conventional livestock and crop farm into a diversified commercial enterprise integrating eco-tourism, green power generation, and enhanced visitor infrastructure, while preserving its core agricultural plots.\n\nIn 1950, the farm was dedicated entirely to agricultural activities. The expansive northern pasture was designated for sheep, while the western perimeter alongside the river was partitioned into plots for fruit trees, soft fruits, and vegetables. In the eastern sector, the site accommodated a barn, a main farmhouse, and a chicken enclosure, connected to the primary northern road via an unpaved track.\n\nToday, the landscape exhibits extensive modern additions. The original sheep field has been repurposed into a camping ground, complemented by an array of solar panels in the northeastern quadrant. To cater to visitors, a farm shop and two separate parking lots have been added, and the former track has been upgraded into a paved road system. Architecturally, the barn has been relocated further south, making way for new holiday cottages on its former footprint. Notably, the western crop zones, farmhouse, and chicken enclosure have remained completely unaltered throughout the decades.",
        "tips": [
            "Master directional vocabulary: northern pasture, southeastern portion, along the western perimeter, northeastern quadrant.",
            "Use map-specific passive verbs: repurposed, converted into, replaced by, relocated, expanded, upgraded, paved, remained unaltered.",
            "Use Present Perfect Passive for changes leading to today, and Past Simple for 1950.",
            "Dedicate one paragraph to what changed and another to what stayed the same.",
            "Summarize the bigger functional shift in the overview, not just a list of objects."
        ],
        "examples": [
            {"sentence": "The sheep field is gone and now there is camping and solar panels.", "band": "Band 5", "tip": "Use transformation verbs: 'The sheep area has been replaced by a camping field and solar panels.'"},
            {"sentence": "The sheep field has been converted into a camping field, with solar panels installed in the top right corner.", "band": "Band 7", "tip": "Swap vague position phrases like 'top right corner' for precise compass terms like 'northeastern quadrant'."},
            {"sentence": "The original sheep field has been repurposed into a camping ground, complemented by an array of solar panels in the northeastern quadrant.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Combine related additions into one fluid compound clause."},
            {"sentence": "They moved the barn down and built holiday cottages where the old barn was.", "band": "Band 6", "tip": "Avoid the vague pronoun 'they'; use the passive voice for map descriptions."},
            {"sentence": "Architecturally, the barn has been relocated further south, making way for new holiday cottages on its former footprint.", "band": "Band 9", "tip": "Use connectors like 'making way for' or 'occupying the site of' to phrase structural replacements."}
        ],
    },
]

